    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that formula looks okay but it seems a bit too static. i'm mostly worried about the \"hidden\" variables—like when the supplier says they'll ship on time but then something unexpected happens and the shipment just disappears or is delayed without warning. how do i account for those kinds of unpredictable, sudden disruptions in the safety stock calculation?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To account for these unpredictable disruptions, you can introduce a buffer factor, often referred to as \"downtime factor\" or \"disruption factor\". This can be represented as a percentage (e.g., 5%) that represents the likelihood of lead-time disruption. Multiply this factor by the lead time to get the weighted lead time, then plug this value into the initial formula. Alternatively, you can use a more complex model like the Stochastic Lead Time (SPLT) to incorporate uncertainty into the lead time calculation.",
      "source": "target",
